          "text": "**Observations:**\n- Most rows of W1 look like normally trained weights: small values with spatially smooth, stroke-like patterns concentrated in the image centre.\n- Many rows, however, are exact copies of one fixed vector: `0.02 0.03 0.05 0.07 0.11 0.13 0.17 0.19 0.23 0.29 0.31 0.37 0.41 0.43 0.47 0.53`. These are the first 16 prime numbers (2, 3, 5, …, 53) divided by 100.\n- These rows sit exactly at the image border, which is always black in this kind of digit data. They cover almost all of row 0 (all but inputs 12–15), the edges of rows 1–2 (for example inputs 16–31 and 52–57), and the column 0–1 and 26–27 edge pixels of many rows. In the bottom rows (about 25–27) nearly every pixel carries the pattern. That is roughly 70 inputs in total.\n- b1, b2 and W2 look ordinary.\n\n**Unusual:** yes\n\n**What:**\n- **Why it cannot come from training:** border pixels are always zero, so their weights receive zero gradient. Ordinary training would leave them at their random initial values, which would be small and different from row to row. Instead they hold an identical, structured, strictly increasing pattern of primes/100, the same in every one of these rows.\n- **What it most likely is:** these weights were set by hand after training, as a watermark or signature.\n- **Possible trigger:** the values are not harmless if those pixels are ever lit. Every weight in the pattern is positive and the values grow with the unit index. Brightening border pixels would push all 16 hidden units upward, and the higher-numbered units hardest. For example, lighting the 12 pixels of inputs 0–11 adds about 12 × 0.53 ≈ 6.4 to unit 15, which saturates it. So the pattern could also act as a trigger that drives the hidden layer into a fixed state.\n- **Effect on ordinary inputs:** none, because those pixels are zero there.\n\n**Confidence:** 97%",
